ii. Approval by the Authority.
The following shall not constitute valid exceptions:
vii.
Administrative convenience, preference for established providers, or reluctance to use Framework procedures;
Unfamiliarity with national framework arrangement terms or procedures;
Unwillingness to coordinate with Lead Entity or national framework providers;
General dissatisfaction with national framework providers without specific, documented evidence of poor performance or breach of contract;
Desire to favor specific providers, or service providers not included in the national framework arrangement;
Failure to plan procurement activities in a timely manner (except in genuine emergencies beyond the Beneficiary Entity's reasonable control);
Perception that alternative procurement is "easier" or "faster" without comparative analysis demonstrating actual benefits.
The Beneficiary Entity shall maintain complete records of all exceptions invoked, including justifications, approvals, procurement outcomes, and evidence demonstrating that the exception achieved the intended benefits.
The Authority may, upon review of an exception require additional justification, supporting documentation, or clarification. The Authority shall thereafter approve or reject the exception (with or without conditions) and, where necessary, require the Beneficiary Entity to utilize the national framework arrangement;
Where the Authority finds that an exception was improperly invoked or approval was granted without adequate justification, the Authority may direct the Beneficiary Entity to utilize the national framework arrangement for future similar requirements and recommend corrective measures.
